====================================================================================================
0. the pipeline, checked before anything is trained
====================================================================================================
UNet(1, 3, 7, [16, 32, 64, 128]): 170k parameters; (batch, 3, 256) in -> (batch, 7, 256) logits out, so every sample answers for itself
cross-entropy with ignore_index=-1: a flat guess costs log 7 = 1.946, and a window in which every sample is a pause costs nan - not 0 - so those windows are left out of training and validation, where they could teach nothing and a single NaN step would have reset every weight: 25 of 155 training and 17 of 157 validation windows are the long pauses before the first activity or after the last one
130 training windows on 2 recordings, 140 validation windows, 161 evaluation windows on the test users - no overlap, no padding, the last window of a recording slid back so that every sample is answered once, always from a full window of real signal
the search chooses from 24 orientations; the phone will be pretend-worn in 3 (2 axis-aligned, which a candidate can undo exactly, 1 continuous, which none can), and the same setting is chosen on the validation users only

====================================================================================================
1. the baselines, before any network
====================================================================================================
always answering standing: accuracy 0.176, macro F1 0.043
nearest class average of (mean, sd) of each axis, one answer per 256-sample window: accuracy 0.608, macro F1 0.505; per class walk 0.08, upst 0.00, down 0.70, sitt 0.79, stan 0.83, lyin 0.92, tran 0.21
      the class averages come from walk 34, upst 18, down 21, sitt 9, stan 13, lyin 10, tran 2 training windows that hold a single class. A transition runs 194 samples at the median against a 256-sample window, so its average is built from the fewest windows of the seven, and it is the answer a window that straddles a boundary - 9.9% of the test windows - drifts towards, because a boundary is the loudest thing in it
-> standing is 17.6% of the labelled test samples, all the first baseline has to offer; the second reads a whole window at once, the way the app will, and can only ever give one answer for it - the network answers each sample of the window separately

